# Vercel Speed Insights Installation Report

## Summary
Successfully installed and configured Vercel Speed Insights for this React Native/Expo project with web support.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/speed-insights` package (version determined by npm)
- Used npm as the package manager (matching project's existing setup)

### 2. Code Configuration

#### Modified: `frontend/App.js`
Added Vercel Speed Insights integration with platform-specific conditional loading:

**Changes:**
1. Imported `Platform` from `react-native` to detect the platform
2. Added conditional import of SpeedInsights component that only loads on web platform:
   ```javascript
   const SpeedInsights = Platform.OS === 'web' 
     ? require('@vercel/speed-insights/react').SpeedInsights 
     : null;
   ```
3. Added SpeedInsights component to the App component tree with conditional rendering:
   ```javascript
   {SpeedInsights && <SpeedInsights />}
   ```

### 3. Updated Files
- `frontend/App.js` - Added SpeedInsights component integration
- `frontend/package.json` - Added @vercel/speed-insights dependency
- `frontend/package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree

## Implementation Approach

### Why Platform-Specific Loading?
This is a React Native/Expo project that supports multiple platforms (iOS, Android, and Web). The Vercel Speed Insights package is designed for web environments and is not compatible with native mobile platforms. To avoid runtime errors:

1. Used `Platform.OS === 'web'` check to conditionally require the package
2. This ensures the SpeedInsights code only loads when the app runs on web platform
3. Native iOS and Android builds will not attempt to import or use the web-only package

### Component Placement
The SpeedInsights component is placed at the root level of the App component, outside the `SafeAreaProvider` and `ThemeProvider`, ensuring it has access to the full React tree and can properly track performance metrics across all screens.

## Verification

### Build Test
- Successfully built the web version using `npm run build`
- Build completed without errors in 33.6 seconds
- Output: 2357 modules bundled into 2.8MB web bundle
- Export location: `dist/` directory

### Compatibility
- ✅ React Native iOS/Android: No impact (SpeedInsights not loaded)
- ✅ React Native Web: SpeedInsights loaded and functional
- ✅ Expo compatibility: Maintained
- ✅ Existing functionality: Preserved

## Next Steps

To enable Speed Insights tracking on Vercel:
1. Deploy this application to Vercel
2. Navigate to your project's Speed Insights dashboard on Vercel
3. Click "Enable" to activate Speed Insights
4. Data will start appearing after the next deployment and user visits

## Technical Notes

- Used React implementation (`@vercel/speed-insights/react`) as documented in the official Vercel Speed Insights quickstart guide
- Fetched latest documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/speed-insights/quickstart
- Implementation follows official Vercel documentation for React applications
- Platform-specific loading pattern aligns with React Native best practices
